1. What is a User?
A user is a person or account that can log in to Linux.
Examples:
•	root (administrator) 
•	ali 
•	zeeshan 
Check your current user:
whoami
Example output:
zeeshan
________________________________________
2. What is a Group?
A group is a collection of users.
Example:
Suppose a company has three employees:
Ali
Ahmed
Zeeshan
Create a group called:
developers
Add all three users to it.
Now instead of giving permissions to each user separately, you can give permissions to the developers group.
________________________________________
Simple Real-Life Example
Imagine a classroom:
•	Students = Users 
•	Class = Group 
If the teacher gives a book to the whole class, every student in that class can use it.
________________________________________
Check Current User
whoami
________________________________________
Check User ID and Groups
id
Example:
uid=1000(zeeshan) gid=1000(zeeshan) groups=1000(zeeshan),27(sudo)
________________________________________
See All Users
cat /etc/passwd
________________________________________
See All Groups
cat /etc/group
________________________________________
Create a New User
sudo useradd ali
Set password:
sudo passwd ali
________________________________________
Create a New Group
sudo groupadd developers
________________________________________
Add User to a Group
sudo usermod -aG developers ali
Explanation:
•	-a = append 
•	-G = supplementary group 
________________________________________
Check User's Groups
groups ali
Example:
ali : ali developers
________________________________________
Remove User from a Group
sudo gpasswd -d ali developers
________________________________________
Change File Owner
sudo chown ali file.txt
________________________________________
Change File Group
sudo chgrp developers file.txt
________________________________________
Check File Owner and Group
ls -l file.txt
Example:
-rw-r--r-- 1 ali developers 100 Jun 4 file.txt
Here:
•	Owner = ali 
•	Group = developers 
________________________________________
Delete a User
sudo userdel ali
Delete user and home directory:
sudo userdel -r ali
________________________________________
Delete a Group
sudo groupdel developers
________________________________________
Important Files
File	Purpose
/etc/passwd	User information
/etc/shadow	Encrypted passwords
/etc/group	Group information
________________________________________
Most Important Commands for Exams and Practice
Command	Purpose
whoami	Show current user
id	Show user ID and groups
groups	Show groups
useradd username	Create user
passwd username	Set password
userdel username	Delete user
groupadd groupname	Create group
groupdel groupname	Delete group
usermod -aG group user	Add user to group
gpasswd -d user group	Remove user from group
chown	Change owner
chgrp	Change group
ls -l	View owner and group of files
Easy Memory Trick
•	User = One person/account. 
•	Group = Collection of users. 
•	chown = Change Owner. 
•	chgrp = Change Group. 
•	chmod = Change Permissions. 


